---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { profile, languages } from '../data';
---
<Base title="About" description="How a CPA ended up building credit-risk models, and what I do away from work.">
  <article class="wrap about">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        I'm a data scientist in Tel Aviv working on credit risk and fraud. I came to machine learning
        from accounting, and I still think of a model as an entry in someone's books.
      </p>

      <h2>From the ledger to the model</h2>
      <p>
        I started at UHY Israel as a financial auditor, auditing public and private companies, and
        spent time as the controller of a listed financial-services firm. That work teaches you what
        a number goes through before it reaches a report, and what it costs when it's wrong.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 2018 I moved to Dov &amp; Co, a CPA firm, as a software developer and blockchain
        administrator. I built financial software and blockchain applications, and advised clients on
        how digital assets are regulated and taxed. Writing code to automate the work I knew from audits
        led me to data science.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 2020 I completed the Israel Tech Challenge data-science fellowship and joined Fido Credit
        Solutions, where I've built credit-risk models for three international markets, fraud rules,
        uplift models for Collections, forecasts for Finance and, lately, agentic AI workflows that take
        the repetitive parts out of model development.
      </p>

      <h2>What I bring to a team</h2>
      <p>
        A model in lending is a business decision written in code. I own the whole path, from features
        and evaluation to deployment and monitoring. I also explain the result in terms that finance,
        risk and compliance already use: losses, margins, controls and audit trails.
      </p>

      <h2>Away from work</h2>
      <p>
        I play competitive chess in a local community league. It's good training for a risk modeler:
        you're always weighing the move that looks best against the one that's least likely to lose.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also build things on the side. <a href="https://theglobalmin.app">The Global Min</a> is my
        data-science interview-prep app.
      </p>

      <h2>Languages</h2>
      <p>{languages.join(' and ')}.</p>

      <h2>Get in touch</h2>
      <p>
        The best way to reach me is email, at <a href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>{profile.email}</a>.
        I'm also on <a href={profile.linkedin}>LinkedIn</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .about { padding-top: clamp(24px, 5vw, 56px); padding-bottom: 72px; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.3rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  h2 { margin-top: 1.8em; font-size: var(--step-1); }
</style>
